{% block how_it_works %}
## How it works

The two arms are sets of paths: `diff` is resolved per path, and a path that
would fall in both arms raises an error instead of being counted twice. With an
`assignment` table the population is intention-to-treat — every assigned path,
including those with no events (they count as zeros), with events before
`assigned_at` dropped.

The test follows the metric: a pooled z-test with Wilson and Newcombe intervals
for 0/1 metrics, Welch's t-test for per-path totals and values, and a joint
path bootstrap for ratio metrics (an entry with a `denominator`). The
`verdict` reads only the adjusted p-value; the interval is per comparison. The
[A/B Testing guide](/docs/ab-testing) is a walk-through on the bundled synthetic
experiment, `load_ab_test()`.

### Reading a row

The last column, `reading`, says in one sentence what the row's own numbers
support, so a scorecard can be read row by row without re-deriving the rules.
0/1 metrics are read in percentage points (pp), other metrics in their unit;
negative numbers use a plain `-`, so the text can be parsed back.

Every sentence starts with the window the row covers: `over the whole
observation period`, `within 28 days of assignment` (with `horizon`),
`calendar week 2026-03-09` or `days 14-27 since assignment` (period rows). A
period row describes its window, not the experiment: quote the window with the
number.

- **inconclusive** — `"over the whole observation period: interval [-1.07,
  +2.28] pp includes 0 (p 0.48); effects outside it are unlikely; the design
  detects 2.39 pp at alpha 0.05 — to detect 1 pp, about 5.7× the paths are
  needed"`. The interval bounds the effect; the
  `mde` describes the design. The multiplier comes from the design variance: the
  `mde` shrinks with the square root of the paths, so a round effect (1, 2 or 5
  times a power of ten) at or below half the `mde` needs `(mde / effect)²` times
  the paths at the same split. The clause
  is left out when there is no `mde`. With a correction over several rows the
  sentence says `after holm` (or `bonferroni`, `bh`), and the p-value is the
  adjusted one; a row whose interval excludes 0 only before the correction says
  so.
- **increase / decrease** — `"within 14 days of assignment: interval [+1.38,
  +4.12] pp excludes 0 (p < 0.0001); relative +21 %"`.
- **insufficient_data** — `"calendar day 2026-03-02: pooled support 6 <
  min_events 10; estimate shown descriptively"`; a row where no path of either
  arm has the behaviour reads `"no data in either arm"` (`support_warning="no_data"`).
- **descriptive** — `"active population: not randomised, describes who was
  active in the period"`.

Then the row's flags, when set: `exploratory: strata not pre-exposure (declare
strata_pre_exposure=True if they are)`, `window not complete` for a period whose
window the data does not cover, and the `support_warning`. With several metrics
and no strata or periods, each row ends with `corrected within this metric only;
name one decision metric`: the correction never spans metrics, so a decision
needs one metric named before the test. With `correction="bonferroni"` the
sentence quotes the simultaneous interval. Since-assignment bins whose window
closes after the end of data for every path have no population in either arm
and are left out.

